# Byte-fidelity guard for template assets.
#
# assets/ holds files that the wizard's generated prompt tells Claude Code to
# copy VERBATIM into every new project (PowerShell scripts, .github templates,
# git templates). Several of these carry a UTF-8 BOM on purpose: PowerShell
# 5.1 reads a BOM-less UTF-8 file through the system ANSI codepage and mangles
# any Czech diacritics in it. sha256 pins in tests/assets.test.mjs assert the
# exact bytes of the .ps1 scripts against a corrected copy of the KOFOLA
# production source.
#
# Without this file, git's core.autocrlf=true (set for this repo) rewrites
# LF -> CRLF on checkout for anything it thinks is text, which both breaks
# byte-identity with the source and silently reflows any BOM-adjacent bytes.
# That makes the sha256 pins pass or fail depending on checkout state alone,
# not on any real edit to the assets - a guard that flips with the working
# tree is not a guard.
#
# `-text` alone is what suppresses the translation. Deliberately NOT `-diff`:
# marking these binary would render future template edits as "binary files
# differ", and these are exactly the files a human must be able to read in a
# diff before they ship into a customer project.
#
# Historical note on the pins: bootstrap.ps1 and check-drift.ps1 were only ever
# `cp`-ed from the source, so their LF bytes - and therefore their pins - were
# always correct. Generate-ReposMd.ps1 was later rewritten by an editor (to add
# the `@(...)` wrapper) which wrote CRLF, so its pin captured CRLF bytes; that
# is why restoring LF changed only that one pin. Verified: the superseded pin
# 5091c899... is exactly the CRLF hash of today's LF file.
assets/** -text
